<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    APPLIES TO EVERY BUILD, INCLUDING RELEASE.

    Cleartext HTTP is DENIED by default here, and permitted for exactly one
    address: 192.168.137.1, which is where Windows Mobile Hotspot always puts
    the host. That is the lecturer's laptop when the phone has joined its
    hotspot: a two-device private network with no router and no internet path.

    Why this is not a blanket allow:
      base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" keeps the Android default
      (no cleartext) for everything else. A public host, the tunnel domain, or
      any other LAN address still cannot be reached over http from a release
      build. Only the single literal below is exempt.

    Why an address and not a range:
      Android's network security config has no CIDR syntax; it matches domains
      and literals. Listing one deterministic address is tighter than any
      approximation of a range would be, and it matches exactly what
      EndpointResolver.lanCandidates probes.

    This is the SECOND lock. EndpointConfig.isAllowedApiBase independently
    refuses any address that is not a private literal or the HTTPS tunnel, so
    an attacker would have to defeat both the app pin and this file.

    The debug source set additionally permits cleartext broadly for local
    development (app/src/debug/res/xml/network_security_config.xml); that file
    is absent from release builds.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">192.168.137.1</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
